  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 100 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThere is strong confidence in the dry/warm signal carrying thru\nthe remainder of the week. This will translate to daily highs\nthru Saturday approaching or in some locations even topping 70F\ndegrees. Time/height cross sections show moisture remains \nscarce despite a subtle climb in return flow dews, thru the 40s \ntoward 50F by Saturday; that's a positive for keeping RH from \nplummeting toward a heightened fire danger but a looser pgf \nlikewise offers lighter overall wind conditions as well.\n\nNext rain chances come in with peak chances late Saturday-early\nSunday as a cold front and its parent upper trof make approach\nand passage. The NBM peaks 80% chances in the northwest late\nSaturday night, tapering to 40% in the southeast early Sunday\nmorning. The southeast is where what little instability that \ndoes exist is best exhibited, with upward to 200 J/KG2 MUCAPE in\na narrow sliver developing just ahead of the wave; an isolated \nmention of general risk thunder appears in the grids there\n(south/east) along the MO/AR border briefly as a result.\n\nSince moisture and instability is so limited, despite the higher\ncomputable rain chances, storm total average QPF should still be\na quarter inch or less. The main impact of fropa will be a \nnotable spill of cold air into/across the region, with an \nimmediate Sunday nose-dive into the 50s for highs for all but \nour far southeast. Following, brisk/cold highs in the 40s are \nexpected by mid week next as the colder air settles down with \nhigh pressure sourcing from Manitoba across the Great Lakes over\nthis time, driving the cold spill across our area with vigor.",
